{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "Bundler",
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "strict": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "isolatedModules": true
  },
  "include": [
    "packages/schemas/generated/**/*.ts",
    "apps/workspace/src/**/*.ts",
    "apps/workspace/src/**/*.tsx",
    // Round 12d: the Glass's spec trees were outside `tsc --noEmit` entirely, so a spec could
    // reference a helper that did not exist and `make typecheck` stayed green — found exactly
    // that way, when a deleted helper surfaced only as a `ReferenceError` at run time against a
    // real stack. `apps/harness/keyboard` was already included; the Glass's own equivalents were
    // not, which is an asymmetry rather than a deliberate exemption.
    //
    // **Only `live/` is added here, and the rest is a disclosed gap, not an oversight.** Adding
    // `keyboard/`, `perf/` and `a11y/` as well surfaces four *pre-existing* type errors
    // (`query-builder-harness.tsx`'s `"thing"` PoleClass, `registryCoverage.test.ts`'s
    // possibly-undefined argument, and two `window.__PERF__` accesses with no declaration).
    // None is this round's to fix, and silencing them to widen the include would be worse than
    // saying so: they are recorded in `07_BUILD_PLAN.md` as their own item.
    "apps/workspace/live/**/*.ts",
    "apps/harness/src/**/*.ts",
    "apps/harness/src/**/*.tsx",
    "apps/harness/keyboard/**/*.ts",
    "apps/harness/keyboard/**/*.tsx"
  ]
}
